From: Yusuke Shimizu - Workshop Asahi <asahi.00@ws-asahi.net>
Subject: [PATCH] bfdio: convert paths with the process ACP, not the CRT locale

_bfd_real_fopen() on MinGW converts the incoming (multi-)byte path to
a wide string with MultiByteToWideChar() before calling _wfopen(), and
picks the conversion code page from the CRT locale via
___lc_codepage_func().

That source is wrong.  On Windows, the bytes that reach a program
through its narrow argv are by construction encoded in the PROCESS
ANSI code page: the OS converts the wide command line with the ACP,
which is the system code page in the classic regime (e.g. 932 on
Japanese Windows) and 65001 when the executable carries an embedded
activeCodePage=UTF-8 manifest.  The CRT locale's code page, however,
is resolved from the USER locale by setlocale(..., "") and stays 932
on a Japanese system regardless of any manifest.

The mismatch is invisible as long as both regimes agree (classic
builds: argv bytes 932, locale cp 932 - works; ASCII-only paths: all
code pages agree - works) but breaks exactly when a UTF-8 manifest is
embedded and a path contains multibyte characters: UTF-8 bytes get
decoded as Shift-JIS, GetFullPathNameW()/_wfopen() receive a mangled
wide path, and every bfd-level open of such a path fails - observed as
ld "cannot find <correct-looking path>" for startfiles and libraries
under a Japanese-named prefix, while the (UTF-8-aware) GCC driver and
compiler proper handle the very same paths fine.

Use GetACP() instead: it reports the actual encoding of argv in both
regimes, keeping classic ANSI builds byte-for-byte identical in
behaviour and making UTF-8-manifest builds correct.

--- a/bfd/bfdio.c
+++ b/bfd/bfdio.c
@@ -122,11 +122,18 @@
     }
 
 #ifdef __MINGW32__
-#if !HAVE_DECL____LC_CODEPAGE_FUNC
-  /* This prototype was added to locale.h in version 9.0 of MinGW-w64.  */
-  _CRTIMP unsigned int __cdecl ___lc_codepage_func (void);
-#endif
-  const unsigned int cp = ___lc_codepage_func ();
+  /* wazamono: use the process ANSI code page, not the CRT locale's.
+     Windows converts the wide command line to narrow argv bytes with
+     the process ACP (GetACP ()), so the ACP is by construction the
+     encoding of every path that reaches us through argv - both in the
+     classic regime (ACP = system code page, e.g. 932) and when an
+     embedded activeCodePage manifest makes the process run with
+     ACP = UTF-8 (65001).  ___lc_codepage_func () instead reports the
+     CRT locale's code page, which setlocale (..., "") resolves from
+     the USER locale (932 on Japanese Windows) regardless of the
+     manifest; under the UTF-8 regime that mis-decodes multibyte paths
+     here and every bfd-level open of such a path fails.  */
+  const unsigned int cp = GetACP ();
 #else
   const unsigned int cp = CP_UTF8;
 #endif
